## Dataset

**Primary:** `data/raw/crop_weather_records.csv`

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Rows | 31,365 |
| Columns | year, district, state, crop_type, season, temperature_c, rainfall_mm, humidity_pct, yield_t_per_ha |
| States | 19 (Andhra Pradesh, Assam, Bihar, Chhattisgarh, Gujarat, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jharkhand, Karnataka, Kerala, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Punjab, Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand, West Bengal) |
| Districts | 229 |
| Crops | Groundnut, Maize, Ragi, Rice, Wheat |
| Seasons | Kharif, Rabi |
| Year range | 1980 – 2017 |
| Yield range | 0.004 – 21.4 t/ha (mean: 1.65 t/ha) |

**Fallback:** `data/sample/crop_weather_synthetic.csv` — a reproducible synthetic
dataset used automatically when the raw file is absent.

**To use real data**, place a CSV at `data/raw/crop_weather_records.csv` with the
columns listed above. Supported crop/season combinations are defined in
`src/weather_impact/config/crops.py`.

---

## Machine Learning Model

### Algorithm

Random Forest Regressor inside a scikit-learn `Pipeline` with a `ColumnTransformer`
preprocessor (StandardScaler for numerics, OneHotEncoder for categoricals).

### Features

| Feature | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `year` | Numeric | Calendar year of observation |
| `temperature_c` | Numeric | Seasonal average temperature (°C) |
| `rainfall_mm` | Numeric | Seasonal total rainfall (mm) |
| `humidity_pct` | Numeric | Average relative humidity (%) |
| `temp_humidity_index` | Numeric (derived) | temperature × humidity / 100 |
| `dist_crop_mean_yield` | Numeric (derived) | Per district × crop historical mean yield |
| `crop_type` | Categorical | One of 5 crop varieties |
| `season` | Categorical | Kharif or Rabi |
| `district` | Categorical | One of 229 districts |
| `state` | Categorical | One of 19 states |

### Training

- **Split:** 80% train / 20% test, random state 42
- **Target:** `yield_t_per_ha` (clipped at 0 — negative yields are not physical)

### Current Metrics

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| R² | 0.827 |
| Accuracy (100 × (1 − WAPE)) | 85.3 % |
| MAE | 0.242 t/ha |
| RMSE | 0.419 t/ha |
| MAPE | 21.0 % |
| WAPE | 14.7 % |

**Why WAPE over MAPE?** Near-zero yields (e.g. 0.004 t/ha) would cause MAPE to
explode even for small absolute errors. WAPE (Weighted Absolute Percentage Error)
= Σ|actual − predicted| / Σ|actual| is robust to near-zero values and is the
primary accuracy metric throughout the project.

### Retraining

```bash
python scripts/train_model.py
```

Saves `models/saved/yield_model.joblib` (the pipeline) and
`models/saved/yield_model.json` (metadata + metrics). The app reads both on
startup — no restart needed if Streamlit's file watcher is active.

---

## Risk Score

Computed entirely from agronomic crop profiles in
`src/weather_impact/config/crops.py`. No model inference is required.

### Method

For each of the three weather factors:

```
distance = max(0, value − optimal_high)  if above band
         = max(0, optimal_low − value)   if below band
         = 0                              if within band

severity = min(1.0, distance / (optimal_high − optimal_low))

score = 100 × (0.40 × temp_severity
              + 0.35 × rain_severity
              + 0.25 × hum_severity)
```

### Supported Crop Profiles

| Crop | Season | Optimal Temp (°C) | Optimal Rain (mm) | Optimal Humidity (%) | Ref. Yield (t/ha) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Rice | Kharif | 22 – 32 | 800 – 1500 | 60 – 85 | 5.5 |
| Maize | Kharif | 18 – 30 | 500 – 900 | 50 – 75 | 6.0 |
| Maize | Rabi | 16 – 28 | 150 – 350 | 40 – 65 | 5.0 |
| Ragi | Kharif | 20 – 30 | 400 – 800 | 50 – 70 | 2.5 |
| Groundnut | Kharif | 22 – 32 | 450 – 900 | 50 – 75 | 2.0 |
| Groundnut | Zaid | 25 – 35 | 100 – 300 | 40 – 65 | 1.8 |
| Wheat | Rabi | 12 – 25 | 100 – 300 | 40 – 70 | 3.5 |

---

## SHAP Explainability

The project uses `shap.TreeExplainer` applied to the Random Forest's underlying
regressor (extracted from the scikit-learn pipeline). Three distinct SHAP
computations are performed:

### 1. Single-row SHAP (Yield Prediction, What-If, Explainability)

```python
from weather_impact.explainability.shap_explainer import explain_prediction

contribs, base_value = explain_prediction(model, pd.DataFrame([inputs]))
# contribs: DataFrame with columns [feature, value, shap_value]
# base_value: float — model's expected value (dataset mean prediction)
```

One-hot encoded columns (e.g. `cat__crop_type_Rice`) are automatically summed
back to their original feature name (`crop_type`) so users see one value per factor.

### 2. Dataset-level SHAP (Explainability Engine)

```python
# In app.py — cached for 1 hour
ds = _shap_dataset(DEFAULT_MODEL_NAME)
# ds["shap_matrix"]: ndarray (n_samples, n_encoded_features)
# ds["orig_names"]:  list[str] — original feature per encoded column
# ds["X_raw"]:       DataFrame — untransformed feature values
# ds["base_value"]:  float
```

Computed on 400 randomly sampled rows. Used for the global importance bar chart,
dependence scatter, and beeswarm distribution.

### 3. Delta SHAP (What-If Simulator)

```python
shap_baseline = _shap_one(baseline_inputs)   # {feature: shap_value}
shap_scenario = _shap_one(scenario_inputs)
delta = {f: shap_scenario[f] - shap_baseline[f] for f in weather_features}
```

The delta waterfall shows exactly which of the three weather sliders caused the
yield change — not just the direction of the total change.

### Technical Note

Standard `shap.plots` render as matplotlib figures, which break the Plotly-based
UI. This project extracts raw values from the `shap.Explanation` object and
renders all SHAP visualisations using `plotly.graph_objects` with the project's
white-card aesthetic.

